      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How thick should an RV pad be?",
            "answer": "<p>Thicker than a driveway. For motorhomes and loaded trailers we typically pour a reinforced slab over a deep compacted base, and thicken it further where the jacks and tyres sit. We'll spec the right thickness for your rig during the free estimate.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How big does my RV pad need to be?",
            "answer": "<p>Measure your rig and add room to open the door, put the awning out and walk around it. We'll help you size it on site — it's cheaper to pour a few extra feet now than to add on later.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Will an RV pad crack under the weight?",
            "answer": "<p>Not when it's built for it. Cracking on heavy pads almost always comes from a weak base or missing reinforcement, so that's where we spend the effort — compaction, steel and control joints in the right places.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "qa",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can you tie the pad into my existing driveway?",
            "answer": "<p>Yes. We match the elevation and finish so it reads as one surface, and we detail the joint properly so the two slabs can move without fighting each other.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
